WEBVTT

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.557
Spark SQL does not execute your text directly in one simple step.

00:00:04.557 --> 00:00:09.677
It parses the SQL, resolves tables and columns, optimizes the logical plan, and creates a physical plan.

00:00:09.677 --> 00:00:15.257
The physical plan includes operators such as scans, filters, joins, aggregates, and exchanges.

00:00:15.257 --> 00:00:17.882
Exchanges often indicate data movement.

00:00:17.882 --> 00:00:21.942
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:21.942 --> 00:00:28.442
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:28.442 --> 00:00:30.107
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:30.107 --> 00:00:36.082
In a real setting such as a lakehouse processing millions of web events across many files,

00:00:36.082 --> 00:00:41.732
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:41.732 --> 00:00:44.132
before discussing syntax.

00:00:44.132 --> 00:00:49.600
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
